INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Know What''s Running', 'A new test command (58+) shows the name of whatever smoke test is currently running against this server, straight from the same @test hook that already logs it -- handy when watching a sweep from in-game instead of the console.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Quiet Paper Trail for Get and Drop', 'Every get and drop is now written to the day''s log file -- who, what (with its vnum), and which room -- silently, with no live [TAG] echo to anyone online. Pull it up later with log search <name> if a dispute over who had what needs sorting out. Also caught the same capitalization bug from the recent look fixes lurking in two more places (scan and get/drop messages both have their own copy of the same helper) -- fixed there too.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'The Clock Remembers Now', 'The world clock no longer resets to 8:00 AM day one every time the server restarts -- it saves itself every tick and picks right back up where it left off. Caught a nasty bug while building this: our query helper only understands %i for whole numbers, not the usual %d, and would have silently swallowed every save without complaint. Worth remembering for the next feature that writes numbers to the database.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Serious Repo Bug, Found and Fixed', 'Found something worth knowing about: a stray line in .gitignore meant to ignore crash-dump files was accidentally matching our own src/core folder by name, silently keeping gametime.c and zone.c out of the git repository the whole time, even though both were pushed as part of earlier commits. The actual code was never lost -- it was live on the server the whole time -- but the repository itself was missing two real source files. Fixed the ignore rule and both files are now properly tracked. Also added a quiet heartbeat: once every real hour, on the half hour, everyone gets a blank line so a tick is visible without any actual message.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Mobs Learn Right From Wrong', 'Characters now have an alignment (good/evil/neutral, set with set <name> alignment), shown in score as a word rather than a raw number. Aggressive mobs check it before picking a fight: a sufficiently good-aligned target gets left alone instead of attacked on sight. A scoped-down first step toward the original''s full Mobile_Attitude system -- suspicion, greed, malice, hate/fear lists, and the rest are still future work.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Pools, Puddles, and Poor Life Choices', 'A new immortal-only pee command (51+) leaves a puddle on the floor -- purely for flavor, but it plugged into two other systems: a bad enough limb injury now leaves a pool of blood behind too, and a new drink <puddle> command lets anyone sample either one, with a real chance of a (non-lethal) case of poisoning. Repeat the same kind of mess in one room and it grows -- puddle, then pool, then large pool, the color escalating from dim to bright the bigger it gets -- rather than littering the floor with duplicates. Left alone, every puddle shrinks back down a size every tick or so until it''s soaked fully into the ground.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Wandering Mob Bug, and Immortals Get Their Own Entrance', 'Fixed a real bug in mob wandering: the leave/arrive messages were printing a mob''s raw look-up keywords instead of its actual description ("lady stroll walk leaves" instead of anything sensible) -- now reads properly, with the real direction, e.g. "A lady walks to the east." Immortals can also now set their own custom arrival/departure messages with bamfin and bamfout, in place of the default wording -- e.g. "drags $p cross in from the $d" reads correctly for any gender, since $p/$d are filled in with the right pronoun and direction for whoever set it.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'One Edit Command To Rule Them All', 'edroom, edzone, edplayer, edhelp, ednews, edwiznews, and edrules are gone -- everything now goes through a single edit command instead: edit room [vnum], edit zone <n>, edit player <name>, edit help <topic>, edit news <headline>, edit wiznews <headline>, edit rules <n> <title>. Nothing about how any of them work changed, and every level requirement is exactly what it always was -- just one door to knock on instead of seven.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Scripting Arrives: edit trigger', 'Builders (51+) can now attach scripted behavior to a room, mob, or object with edit trigger -- no recompile needed, unlike SneezyMUD''s old spec proc system. Room triggers fire on enter or ambiently at random; mob triggers fire on greet (someone walks in), speech (a matching keyword), death, or random; object triggers fire on get or wear. The script itself is a short list of actions -- echo, echoroom, emote, teleport, give, damage, log -- written in the same line editor news and rules already use. See help trigger for the full rundown. This is a first pass: the action list is deliberately small on purpose, not a general-purpose language.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Shout It From the Rooftops', 'A new shout command reaches every playing character in the game, not just the shouter''s room -- asleep characters don''t hear it, and toggle noshout lets a mortal opt out entirely (an immortal''s shout always gets through regardless). See help shout.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Slow Tick, Fixed', 'Found and fixed a real performance bug in the new scripting system: the random-trigger check was quietly hitting the database once for every single mob and room in the world, every tick, even on servers with no random triggers set up at all. Now it checks once per tick instead, and aitick/the real world clock both run at their proper speed again.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Loot It All At Once', 'get all <container> empties an entire corpse, bag, or chest into your hands in one command instead of naming each item -- get all corpse after a kill is now all it takes.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'News Was Quietly Losing Its Oldest Entries', 'Found and fixed a real bug: both news and wiznews build the whole feed into a fixed-size buffer before showing it a page at a time, and with this many entries now on the books, that buffer was overflowing and silently cutting off everything past a certain point -- including some of the oldest stories. Both are sized generously now, with plenty of room to keep growing.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fountains Are Drinkable Again', 'Fixed a real bug: drink only ever recognized ground puddles (pee/blood), so a real fountain or drink container already sitting in a room -- and there are plenty seeded in the world -- failed with "You don''t see that here to drink." Drink now also resolves any real drink-category object: clean water, no poison, never runs dry.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixtures Get Top Billing', 'Room descriptions now list permanent scenery -- fountains, lampposts, statuary, anything that can''t be picked up -- ahead of ordinary loot and whoever else is standing there, instead of whatever order they happened to load in.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Starter Taste of Scripting', 'Two of SneezyMUD''s old hardcoded behaviors have been reborn as real edit trigger content: the dirty refuse hauler mutters something rude if you say hello nearby (and grumbles unprompted now and then), and a new pickable tangle of thorny brambles scratches whoever grabs it. Both are ordinary database rows now, not compiled-in code -- see edit trigger list mob 33271 or edit trigger list obj 1000001 to see exactly how they''re built.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Browse Prototypes by Number, Not Just Name', 'vnum <room|obj|mob> <pattern> now also accepts a bare vnum or a vnum range (vnum obj 1017, or vnum obj 100-200) to browse prototypes directly by number, alongside its existing name search -- one command instead of three separate list commands.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Game-Wide Switches Get Their Own Room', 'toggle no longer shows or accepts multiplay or any other global, everyone-affecting switch -- those moved entirely to a new gametog command (58+). toggle is now purely about your own personal switches (color, hp in prompt, and the rest).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Bugs Can Be Fixed, Not Just Deleted', 'A new edbug <id> [note] command (59+) marks a filed bug resolved without deleting it -- if the reporter is online right now, they get a live notice, note included. delbug is still there for a report that never deserved to be kept around at all.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Quiet Watcher Joins the Staff', 'A new snoop <name> command (59+) mirrors everything a lower-level player sees and types straight to your own screen -- you cannot snoop anyone your own level or higher, it just fails. Bare snoop (no name) stops it. Covert by design: the target is never told.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Help Files Get a Cleanup', 'Help topics now title-case the command name in their header, render the description in bright white instead of magenta, and drop redundant "Administrator (N+) only" phrasing now that the footer already shows Minimum Level. The /f (format) command in every text editor also indents each paragraph''s first line two spaces.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Exits Now Match the Room', 'The [Exits:] line in look used to always be green -- now it takes on the same color as the room name itself, so a lava cavern''s exits glow red, a forest clearing''s glow green, and so on, instead of clashing with whatever the room actually looks like.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Choose Your Path', 'Character creation now asks for a race (Human, Elf, Ogre, Dwarf, Hobbit, Gnome), a class (Mage, Cleric, Warrior, Thief, Druid, Monk), and an alignment (Good, Neutral, Evil) right after point-buy attributes -- each race and class carries its own stat bonuses and penalties, and class also scales starting hit points. Alignment isn''t just flavor: an aligned aggressive mob will only ever fight the opposite alignment (never its own kind), leaving neutral players alone in combat but occasionally giving them a one-line in-character reaction instead -- a nod of approval from something good, a sneer from something evil.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Snoop Marks Every Mirrored Line', 'snoop used to only prefix the target''s typed commands with "% " -- their own output was mirrored to you completely unmarked, easy to mistake for your own screen. Every mirrored line is now prefixed the same way, commands and output alike.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Build It Live, Then Zonefile Create', 'New builder tool: `zonefile create <zone>` snapshots a zone''s CURRENT live mobs and objects -- load a mob, drop a chest, put something inside it -- into real reset data, so the next boot or periodic reset recreates exactly what you built. It''s safe to re-run: anything already covered is left alone, so deleting one line from the reset data and running it again only fills that gap back in, never duplicating what''s still there. See `help zonefile`.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Bamfin/Bamfout Now Belong to Goto', '`bamfin`/`bamfout` set your custom `goto` teleport messages now, shown to the room you leave and the room you arrive in -- three tokens available: <N> for your name (anywhere in the message), $g for the room''s ground word, and $p for your pronoun. The WALKING move-message feature that used to answer to `bamfin`/`bamfout` is renamed `poofin`/`poofout` (its original name) -- nobody''s custom message was lost in the move. See `help bamfin`/`help poofin`.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Character Creation Explains What Went Wrong', 'A rejected character name used to get one generic message no matter the reason. Now it tells you exactly what''s wrong: too short (under 3 letters), too long (over 15 letters), or containing something other than letters -- each gets its own message.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Nospam Silences Missed Swings', 'A new personal `toggle nospam` (default off) hides "you miss"/"X misses you" combat messages from your own screen -- ported from Sneezy''s AUTO_NOSPAM. It''s checked independently for each side of a swing, so your own toggle only ever affects what YOU see.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Hostnames Instead of Raw IPs', 'Immortal-facing logs and `users` now show a reverse-DNS hostname instead of a bare IP address where one resolves (falls back to the IP otherwise) -- looked up in the background so a slow DNS server never stalls the game.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Every Class Gets Its Own Skill Sheet', 'A new `skills` command shows your class''s full roster of skills/spells, ported from Sneezy''s real discipline lists and organized into three tiers: Combat, <Class> Skills, and Advanced <Class> Skills. Warrior, Thief, Monk, Cleric, and Mage are all populated -- a skill is known once your level reaches its threshold, no separate practice step yet. Actual in-combat mechanics for most individual skills are still on the way; this is the roster/visibility layer.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Edit Player Can Now Change Class and Race', '`edit player` (58+) gained two new fields: 9) Class and 0) Race, alongside the existing level/xp/hp/attributes/gender/title/load-room/handedness. Changes save and sync to an already-connected target immediately, same as every other field.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Armor Now Actually Protects You', 'Worn armor has a real Armor Class now, shown on `score`, and it makes you harder to hit -- subtracted from the attacker''s hit roll in combat. Since the seeded item data never carried a real per-piece AC value, it''s derived from the piece''s weight (heavier = more protection, capped at 30). The to-hit formula also gained a guaranteed hit/miss floor: no stat or gear mismatch, however extreme, can make a hit or a miss completely impossible.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Ordinal Targeting: 2.sword, 3.goblin', 'When more than one thing in a room shares a keyword, "get 2.sword"/"kill 3.goblin" now reach the 2nd/3rd match instead of always the first. Bare names still work exactly as before. Works for get/drop/put/give/wear/remove and attack/kill alike.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Combat Messages No Longer Show Raw Mob Keywords', 'Fighting a mob whose name has several keywords (e.g. "lady stroll walk", matched by look lady/stroll/walk) used to leak that raw keyword list into miss/hit/death messages and even a mob''s own corpse description. All of it now correctly shows the mob''s short description instead ("a lady out for a stroll"). Several smaller color-tag-skip capitalization bugs (mob greet/speech/death triggers, wander/scavenge/aggress messages) were fixed the same pass.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Casting and Praying Need the Right Item', 'Mages and Druids can now `cast <spell>` -- but need a spell component (any item keyworded "component") on hand, consumed on a successful cast. Clerics `pray <spell>` instead, needing a holy symbol (keyworded "symbol") that is NOT consumed. Both check class and level against the spell first. Real per-spell mechanics are still limited (heal/damage-flavored spells work; the rest cast successfully but do nothing yet) -- the full effect system is follow-up work.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Room Listings Stack Identical Things', 'Multiple identical mobs or objects in a room now show as one line with an "(xN)" count instead of repeating the same line over and over -- three gremlins now read "A gremlin is here. (x3)".')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Immortals Ignore Class Restrictions on Skills/Spells', 'Immortals can now `cast` or `pray` any spell in the game regardless of their own class, and `skills` shows every class''s full roster (grouped by class heading) instead of just their own. Level requirements are bypassed the same way. Component/holy-symbol item requirements still apply -- those are an item gate, not a class restriction. Mortals see no change.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Practice with a Guildmaster to Learn Your Discipline', 'Reaching a spell/skill''s level requirement is no longer enough on its own -- you now also need to have practiced that discipline. A new `practice` command, used at a guildmaster mob of your own class, raises your Basic and Advanced discipline percentages (10% per visit). Basic-tier skills/spells need any Basic percentage above 0; Advanced-tier ones need Basic at 95%+ AND some Advanced percentage. `skills` shows both percentages and marks locked entries with the reason. Guildmaster mobs are recognized by the "guildmaster" keyword plus a class match (the old, previously-unused mob.class column now feeds this). Immortals bypass this gate entirely, same as the class/level bypass.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Heal Someone Else, Then Just `continue`', '`pray heal light <target>` (and every other heal-type prayer) can now be aimed at someone else in the room instead of only yourself -- leave the target off to heal yourself as before. A new `continue` command repeats that same heal automatically, round after round, until the target is fully healed or you run out of holy symbols. Speaking of which: holy symbols are now consumed on every successful prayer, just like a mage or druid''s spell components -- no longer a permanent keepsake.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New `balance` Command for Implementors (60+)', 'A new menu-driven `balance class|race <name>` command lets an Implementor tune four gamewide modifiers per class or race: HP multiplier, damage multiplier, to-hit modifier, and AC modifier. Every class/race starts perfectly neutral (1.00x/1.00x/+0/+0) -- nothing changes until someone actually balances one. Saved changes apply immediately, server-wide, no restart needed.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Weapon Depth: Sharp Weapons and Dual Wielding', 'Edged and piercing weapons (swords, axes, daggers, spears, etc) now deal a little extra damage over blunt ones. Also, the "dual wield" skill (Warrior/Thief) now does something: it removes the usual off-hand damage penalty, so a trained dual-wielder swings evenly with both hands instead of favoring their main hand.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: Leveling Up Now Actually Raises Your HP', 'Found while testing combat depth: leveling up used to only raise your level number -- your max HP (and every limb''s own HP) stayed exactly where it started, forever, making even a high-level character just as fragile as a fresh one. Leveling up now properly raises your max HP and fully heals you, the reward it was always supposed to be.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Doors Can Be Trapped Now', 'A Thief who knows "set trap (door)" can rig a trap on any closed door with the new `settrap <direction>` command; "disarm trap" safely removes one with `disarmtrap <direction>`. Walk through a rigged door without knowing "detect trap" and it springs, hurting a random limb -- one use only. A Thief who DOES know "detect trap" spots it and steps around unharmed, leaving it rigged for whoever comes next.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Buffs and Debuffs: The New Affects System', 'A new general-purpose "affects" system tracks temporary buffs and debuffs with their own countdown, separate from combat. First one live: `pray sanctuary` wraps you (or an ally) in a shimmering aura that halves incoming damage for a while, then fades on its own with a "wears off" message. Check what''s currently affecting you any time with the new `affects` command.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: Reconnecting Could Leave Your Limbs Fragile', 'Found while testing the affects system: reconnecting to an already-created character reset your limbs'' toughness back to a brand-new level-1 character''s, no matter how strong you''d actually grown -- making a veteran just as easy to dismember as a newbie right after logging back in. Limbs are now properly resized to match your real strength on every fresh login.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Friendlier Unknown-Command Message', 'Typing a command that does not exist no longer replies with the terse "Huh?!" -- it now says "Command not found, maybe submit an idea if you believe TobinMUD should have it."')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Grimhaven is Now Tobin City', 'Every mention of "Grimhaven" across rooms, mobs, objects, and zone data (any capitalization) has been renamed to "Tobin City" to match the game''s actual setting.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Six New Commands: consider, examine, sip, show, tell, whisper', '`examine <target>` is a synonym for "look at" something. `consider <target>` (or `consider self`) sizes up a fight before you start one. `sip <liquid>` tastes a puddle or fountain with much less risk than a full `drink`. `show <item> <person>` holds an item up for someone to see without giving it up. `tell <name> <message>` reaches anyone playing anywhere; `whisper <name> <message>` is the same but room-only, with bystanders only knowing a conversation happened.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New Implementor Toy: egotrip blast', 'A new `egotrip blast <target>` command (60+) hits a target with a non-lethal bolt of lightning, halving their current HP (never below 1). The original Sneezy egotrip''s other eleven subcommands aren''t here yet -- they depend on systems Tobin hasn''t built (disease, garble, mob AI hate/aggro, and more).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Combat Messages No Longer Show Raw Damage Numbers', 'Regular combat, spell, and trap messages now read like "You stab a messenger''s left finger." instead of "...for 4 damage!" -- cleaner flavor text for everyone. Immortals still see the number (useful for testing and balancing); mortals don''t.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Look At Someone To See What They''re Wearing', '`look <person>` now shows their worn equipment (body slots and both hands) right after their description, the same listing the `equipment` command shows you for yourself.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New Autoloot Toggle', '`toggle autoloot` now exists: when on, defeating an opponent (or instakilling one as an immortal) automatically pulls everything out of their corpse and into your own inventory, instead of leaving it there to loot by hand.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Major Limbs Are Now Fatal, and Bigger Targets Get Hit More', 'Losing your head, neck, waist, or torso to combat damage is now instant death -- losing an arm, leg, finger, or foot still is not. Taking off the neck also takes the head with it. Which limb gets hit is no longer a flat coin-flip across all thirteen either: a torso is a much bigger target than a finger, so it (and the other bigger limbs) gets hit far more often, mirroring real Sneezy''s own body-part weighting.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New Builder Tool: stat (55+)', '`stat obj|mob|room <vnum>` dumps every single field of that prototype''s row, plus its exits (for a room) or its hitroll/damroll/AC affects (for an object). Unlike `vnum`, which searches by name, `stat` needs an exact vnum -- but shows everything about it once you have one.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Immortals No Longer Take Damage in Combat', 'Landing a hit on an immortal still works exactly as before (verb, messaging, and all) -- it just always deals zero damage now. You can still spar with an immortal for testing purposes; you just can''t actually hurt one.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'stat Speaks Plain English Now', '`stat` no longer dumps raw numbers for the columns that are really flags or lookups: an object''s wear slots and a mob''s action flags now list as readable names ("[ SENTINEL ] [ SCAVENGER ]"), a mob''s class and race show as text ("Cleric", "GOBLIN") instead of their raw codes, and a room exit''s direction, door type, and condition read as words ("dir=north door=Door cond=Closed") instead of numbers. Faction/fact_perc are dropped from the mob dump entirely -- factions are not and will not be supported. The twelve raw attribute columns seeded on every mob are also trimmed down to just the six Tobin actually uses (strength, constitution, dexterity, intelligence, wisdom, charisma); the other six are real Sneezy data but nothing reads them, so showing them would be misleading.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A save Command, and Quitting Now Saves You Too', 'A new `save` command persists your character on demand -- useful reassurance mid-session. More importantly, quitting (`quit!`) or dying now automatically saves everything first, closing a real gap: taking damage in a fight was never written to the database until the fight actually ended, so a quit or a disconnect mid-battle could quietly roll your HP back to whatever it was last saved at. That state is now captured the moment you leave, one way or another.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'stat Decodes Even More Now', '`stat obj` now shows `type` and `action_flag` as readable names instead of raw numbers, and `stat room` does the same for `sector` and `room_flag` -- the same decoding `look` and the room/object editors already relied on internally, just not previously surfaced by `stat`.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'toggle Sorted Into Categories', '`toggle`''s listing is now grouped under three headings -- Preferences, Prompt, and Communication -- instead of one flat list, making it quicker to find the switch you want as more of them get added.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Few More Huh?!s Silenced', 'Found five more spots that still answered with the old terse "Huh?!" instead of the friendlier unknown-command message: casting or praying as the wrong class, `purge linkdead` below level 58, and a Thief-only door trap command used by someone who never learned the skill. All five now match the rest of the game.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'rent Works Now: The Safe Way to Log Off', 'A new `rent` command stores your belongings and ends your session cleanly, the recommended way to leave the game -- simply dropping your connection has always carried a small element of risk. Rent out for a while and you will find yourself healed up when you return, roughly in proportion to how long you were away. Renting is refused mid-fight. Per-item storage cost and restricting rent to inns are not in yet -- both need systems that do not exist yet (money, and a decision about which rooms count as inns).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'quit! Is Now the Risky Way to Leave', 'Now that `rent` exists as the safe way to log off, plain `quit!` has earned its reputation: everything you are carrying, wearing, or holding spills onto the floor right where you typed it, both you and anyone else in the room are told, and there it stays until someone -- possibly not you -- picks it back up. Gold is not affected, since there is no money system yet to have any.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Help Gets a Real Front Door', 'Two new topics -- `help playing` and `help administration` -- are now the front door to the whole help system: a first-timer sees a one-time nudge toward `help playing` right at character creation, and both `help` and `wizhelp`''s own footers point newcomers there too. `playing` walks a new player through looking around, talking, fighting, classes and skills, and the safe (`rent`) versus risky (`quit!`) way to leave. `administration` explains the immortal level ladder (51 through 60) by WHY each tier sits where it does, not just what unlocks -- including why the debug tools (`hurtlimb`, `aitick`, `stat`, `balance`, `egotrip`, `test`) exist at all: real combat, world ticks, and decay are slow and random by design, and these make them instant and deterministic for testing. Alongside that, every command that had gone entirely without a help entry -- `stat`, `save`, `rent`, `cast`, `pray`, `practice`, `skills`, `affects`, `consider`, `continue`, `examine`, `show`, `sip`, `tell`, `whisper`, `balance`, `egotrip`, `settrap`, `disarmtrap`, `hurtlimb`, `aitick`, `immort`, `test` -- now has one. Found and fixed a real bug chasing this down: the old edroom/edzone/edplayer/edhelp/ednews/edwiznews/edrules -> edit-<noun> rename from Session 21 was never actually safe to re-run -- it would collide with itself and abort partway through on a second deploy. Fixed for good.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'goto guildmaster/rent/surplus: Directions, Not Transfers', '`goto` gained three mortal-usable landmark forms: `goto guildmaster` reports walking directions to the nearest guildmaster of your own class, `goto rent` to the inn, and `goto surplus` to the surplus store -- none of these teleport you, they just tell you the shortest real way there, by actual room exits. Standing right there already just says so. Every other form of `goto` (a room vnum, or another player''s name) is still immortal-only, and still teleports as before.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Character Creation: Choose Race and Class First, and See Who You Are Becoming', 'Two changes to new-character creation. First, you now choose your race and class BEFORE allocating attributes, not after -- so your point-buy choices can be made knowing who you already are. Second, the race and class screens no longer show raw stat bonuses ("+2 Dex, -4 Con"); each entry is now a short, evocative description of what that race or class is actually like to play. The real mechanics behind the scenes have not changed at all -- only how they are presented before you have even started playing.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'stat player: See Everything About a Character', '`stat` now has a fourth form: `stat player <name>` dumps a player''s identity, level/experience/HP/alignment, and attributes, decoded the same way the object/mob/room forms already were -- class, race, and gender as readable words, plus an alignment tier alongside the raw number. Also fixed a real dispatch bug found while testing this batch of changes: typing the exact command `set` was occasionally landing on `settrap` instead, because of how commands are matched by table order -- fixed for good.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'open door <direction> Works Now', '`open`/`close` now accept the word "door" in front of a direction (`open door north`, `close door east`), matching the original game''s documented phrasing -- previously only the bare direction form worked. A bare `open door` with no direction opens the room''s one door, if it has exactly one.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Command List Reordered: Mortal Commands First', 'The `help`/`wizhelp` command tables were fully reordered so every mortal-usable command now sorts ahead of every immortal-only one -- less chance of an immortal fat-fingering a mortal command''s abbreviation into an immortal one by accident. Found and fixed two real abbreviation collisions while at it: `set` was occasionally landing on `settrap` instead, and after `goto` became mortal-visible, `g` started reaching it instead of `get`. Further alphabetizing within each tier is on hold pending a follow-up decision.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Game Clock and Regen Now Keep True Real-Time Pace', 'Fixed a timing bug in the server''s core pulse scheduler (`game_loop.c`): every periodic system -- HP regen, combat rounds, the game clock, mob AI, zone aging, puddle decay -- was gated by how many times the main loop happened to iterate, not by real elapsed time. Since a busy socket lets the loop iterate far faster than its normal 100ms tick, heavy connection traffic could make these systems fire much more often than intended (found while chasing a flaky trigger-damage test: an HP regen tick was firing within 1.5 real seconds instead of its intended ~5). The scheduler now tracks real wall-clock time directly, so every pulse-based system keeps its documented pace regardless of how busy the server is.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Command Table Alphabetized -- With Zero Change to What You Type', 'The command table is now alphabetized within each tier, finishing the reorder that was left on hold. Mortal commands still come first as a block, movement still sits pinned at the very top (n/e/s/w/u/d must always mean movement), and everything else is now in plain A-to-Z order -- so finding a command in the source is no longer a hunt. Crucially, NOT ONE abbreviation changed: `sa` still says, `sc` still scores, `dr` still drops, `a` still attacks, `c` still closes. That is not a hopeful claim -- the reorder was generated and then verified by a script that resolves all 432 possible abbreviations at every level against both the old and new tables and diffs them, and it reports zero differences. Sixteen pairs in the mortal tier and one in the immortal tier are deliberately left out of strict alphabetical order because pure A-to-Z would have handed a shared abbreviation to the wrong command (`say` must precede `save` so `sa` still speaks); each is marked in the source with the abbreviation it protects. Also corrected some long-stale comments in that file that claimed `c` reached `color` and `h` reached `help` -- `close` and `hit` had quietly owned those for a while.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Practice System Redesigned: Three Disciplines and Practice Points', 'The practice system has been rebuilt from the ground up. Players now earn practice points when they level up (random 6-8 base, scaled by Wisdom via the `balance wisdom` modifier). Points are spent at guildmasters to raise discipline percentages -- each point buys a random 1-2%. Three discipline tiers now exist: Basic (level-51 guildmasters), Combat (level-80 guildmasters), and Advanced (level-100 guildmasters). Advanced practice is locked until both Basic and Combat hit 100%. The `practice` command now accepts batch syntax (`practice combat 7` to spend multiple points at once). New mortal landmark: `goto combat` gives walking directions to the nearest Combat guildmaster. Combat-tier skills now require some Combat discipline before they can be used, just as Basic and Advanced skills already did. All three percentages are shown in `skills` output.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Practice System Polish: Status Always Visible, Smarter Aliases', 'Four small follow-ups to yesterday''s practice system redesign, found by playtesting. Bare `practice` (no arguments) now always shows your own discipline percentages and practice points, even with no guildmaster in the room -- it is your own character sheet, not something you should need to ask a guildmaster for. When a guildmaster IS present, the status line now only invites training in the discipline that particular guildmaster actually teaches, instead of suggesting all three regardless of tier. `practice <yourclassname>` (e.g. `practice warrior`) now works as a synonym for `practice basic`, matching how `skills` already labels that tier by class name ("Warrior Skills"). And a new `goto <classname>` (e.g. `goto thief`) gives walking directions to that NAMED class''s own Basic guildmaster, not just your own class -- handy for immortals and curious players checking on another class''s trainer without switching characters.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`skills`/`bug`/`idea`/`rules` Now Page Their Output', 'These four commands could print more than a screenful in one shot with no way to slow it down -- `skills` especially, now that it lists three discipline percentages plus every tier. All four now go through the same pager `news`/`wiznews` already use: one screen at a time, ENTER for more, Q to stop. `skills` for an immortal (every class''s full roster) and the outstanding `bug`/`idea` lists benefit the most, but any of the four can grow past one screen as the game''s content grows, so all were switched over together.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Real Per-Skill Proficiency: Learn By Doing, Sneezy-Style', 'Discipline percentages (Basic/Combat/Advanced) have only ever gated ACCESS to a whole tier -- once unlocked, every spell in that tier always worked. That is no longer the whole story. Every individual skill and spell now has its OWN 0-100% proficiency, separate from the discipline gate, that climbs with actual use -- the classic "learn by doing" mechanic, ported from Sneezy''s own system. A freshly-accessible spell starts barely competent (1%) and climbs toward a ceiling set by your discipline percentage for that tier every time you attempt it, win or lose -- so practicing the discipline raises the CEILING, and using the skill climbs TOWARD it. Higher Wisdom softens the diminishing-returns curve, letting gains stay likely further into the climb. Proficiency now genuinely matters: `cast`/`pray` roll against it for every attempt (a low-proficiency spell often fizzles -- "You fumble the casting..." -- a well-practiced one rarely does), and `settrap`/`disarmtrap` and dual wield hook in the same way. `skills` now shows each known skill''s proficiency in brackets, e.g. "bash [34%]". Immortals are unaffected -- they still bypass every gate outright.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`practice <discipline>` Now Shows the Skill List Anywhere', '`practice combat` used to require standing at a Combat guildmaster just to attempt spending a point, and refused outright everywhere else -- not very useful for checking your own progress. Bare `practice <discipline>` (no count) is now a look-anywhere status view: it lists every skill/spell in that ONE discipline along with each one''s own individual proficiency percentage, no guildmaster required. Standing in front of the matching guildmaster adds a reminder to actually spend points there. Only an EXPLICIT count (`practice combat 5`) still spends practice points, and that form still needs the right guildmaster present, unchanged. Net effect: `practice basic`/`practice combat`/`practice advanced` are now readable any time, and only spending requires the trip.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`who` Now Reports Active Links, Linkdead, and Total Players', '`who` always ends with a summary line now: how many connections are actively linked, how many characters are sitting linkdead in the world (disconnected without a clean `quit!`/`rent`), and the total. Shows regardless of any filter (`who imm`/`who mort`/`who <name>`) since it''s a server-health stat, not a scoped listing. Built while investigating a pre-existing connection-handling issue (see the relevant TODO entry) to make linkdead accumulation directly observable instead of guessed at -- `purge linkdead` (Administrator+) already existed to clean them up, this just makes it obvious when there''s something to clean.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Connection Investigation Closed (False Alarm) + a Real Output-Buffering Fix', 'The "connection silently stalls" issue chased for most of today turned out not to be a server bug at all -- it was a test script correctly taking 45+ seconds to run (creating five characters, each needing about nine slow-by-design network round trips) while the diagnostic scripts checking on it were only willing to wait 15-25 seconds before calling it stuck. Re-run with a realistic timeout, the full test passed clean. While chasing it, though, a real (if apparently never-yet-triggered) bug in the networking code got found and fixed: outgoing data could, in rare circumstances, be silently dropped if a connection''s send buffer was momentarily full, with neither side ever finding out. Every connection now has a proper backlog that retries automatically until the data actually goes out, including across a `copyover`.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Boxed Account Menu + a New Login Banner', 'Reaching the account menu used to dump your full character list immediately, with a repeated block of C/N/D/X/Q instructions underneath every single time. It now opens with a clean boxed menu instead -- just the five letter options -- and only lists your characters by name once you actually press C, at which point it shows them numbered and asks which one to connect. A single-character account still connects straight through on C, same as before. The connection screen ahead of login also has a new look: a fortress gate now stands above the TobinMUD logo, greeting every new arrival before they even give an account name.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Boxed Character Creation Screens', 'The revealed character list at the account menu is now boxed too, with each number in bright cyan. New character creation got the same treatment: the race, class, and alignment screens each now present their full option list inside a bordered box, with the option numbers colorized to match. The attribute point-buy screen is boxed now too.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Long Command Output Now Pages Automatically', 'Any command whose reply runs past about 20 lines now shows it a page at a time (ENTER for more, Q to stop) instead of dumping it all at once -- the same behavior `news`/`wiznews`/`skills` already had. Covers `help`, `wizhelp`, `who`, `users`, `stat`, `inventory`, looking inside a well-stocked container, `log list`, and `edit trigger list`. Short output is completely unaffected -- no pager prompt appears unless there is actually more than one page to show.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`pee` Now Takes a Liquid Type', 'Bare `pee` still works exactly as before. `pee <liquid>` -- water, wine, beer, or acid so far, abbreviations welcome -- leaves a puddle of that liquid instead. A second pee of the same liquid grows the existing puddle bigger; a different liquid starts its own puddle alongside it.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New: Define Your Own Command Aliases', '`alias <name> <expansion>` lets you set up your own shortcuts -- `alias k kill` means typing `k orc` sends `kill orc`. Bare `alias` lists what you have, `alias <name>` shows one, `alias remove <name>` deletes one. Aliases live on your account and follow you to every character you play, but mortal and immortal aliases are kept separate -- an immortal alias only works while playing an immortal character, even on the same account. Up to 20 per tier. See `help alias`.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Gold and Shops Have Arrived', 'Killing something now has a payoff: defeated creatures hand their killer gold on the spot, scaled to their level. Check your purse with `score`. All 264 of the game''s original shops are open for business too -- find a shopkeeper, `list` their wares, `buy <item>` or `sell <item>` (only certain goods, matching what each shop actually deals in). Every shop keeps its own personality in its buy/sell messages, same as the original game, just counted in gold now instead of talens. Not built yet: an in-game shop editor, and the old seeded piles of treasure lying around the world do not yet convert to gold when picked up -- both are on the list.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Treasure Piles Now Pay Out', 'Those old piles of gold scattered around the world -- treasure hoards, quest rewards, that sort of thing -- actually do something now. Pick one up and its worth goes straight into your purse instead of sitting in your inventory as a prop; check `score` to see it land.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Kinder Way to Close Up Shop', '`shutdown` is here, Implementor only. Bare `shutdown` counts down from 5 seconds, warning everyone along the way; `shutdown 120` (or any number of seconds) does the same over a longer window without freezing the game for anyone in the meantime. Every connected character is saved right before the world actually closes. Started one by mistake? `shutdown cancel` calls it off.'),
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Shop Listings Are Numbered', '`list` at a shop now numbers every item alongside its price. `buy 3` buys the third thing listed, same as typing its name -- whichever is quicker.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'The Mystery Tag Solved', 'A few old items and rooms had a stray `<h>` sitting in their text where a name was supposed to go -- leftover from the original game''s template system, never carried over to this port. It now fills in as "TobinMUD" wherever it appears, the way it always meant to. A handful of other leftover mentions of the old game''s name, scattered through a book, a couple of rooms, and an item description, are now TobinMUD too.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`wipe` Arrives -- Administrator Only', 'The most permanent command in the game is here: `wipe <name> <password>` erases a character for good, and `wipe account <name> <password>` takes the whole account with it, every character on it included. It needs the wipe master password to run at all (ask whoever holds it), and only reaches someone below your own level. There is no undo, so it earns its own confirmation: getting the password wrong just fails quietly, no second-guessing needed on our end.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Dig Your Own Way', '`dig <direction>` is here for builders. Walk into a direction with no exit yet and it creates a brand new room right then, wires it back to where you were standing, and steps you straight through -- picking the lowest free room number in your own zone automatically. The new room starts bare; `edit room` gives it real shape.'),
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Account Editing for Admins', '`edit account <name>` lets an Administrator rename any account, reset its password, or see who''s on it and at what level. Every change lands immediately, no Save step to remember.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Sickness Is Now a Thing', 'Drink from a puddle on the ground and you might come away with more than a poison scare -- there''s now a 15% chance of catching a Cold, the Flu, Food Poisoning, or (worst luck) the Plague. A disease saps a little HP every so often while it runs its course, then wears off on its own -- or get it cured at a hospital, see below. Immortals are immune.'),
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Hospitals Open Their Doors', 'Six real hospitals are now staffed and ready -- Tobin City, Amber, Logrus, Brightmoon, a field medic''s post, and Xanesla. Walk up to the doctor and `list` to see what ails you (damaged limbs, active diseases), then `buy <#>` to be cured on the spot for gold. Lost track of where the nearest one is? `goto hospital` points the way from anywhere in the world, same as `goto rent` or `goto surplus`.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Component and Commodity Shops Are Finally Stocked', 'Mages and Druids: Camron''s Components (Market Place) now actually sells spellcasting components, so `cast` works again with something to buy rather than nothing to find. Katherine''s Commodities in Brightmoon, plus sister shops in Logrus, Xanesla, and Amber, now carry real metal bars and ingots, and Tuvar''s hides-and-herbs shop is stocked with real animal hides. All buyable and re-sellable, same as any other goods.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Bulletin Boards Are Live', 'The boards mounted on walls across the world finally do something. `read` at one lists what''s posted; `read <#>` shows a message in full. `write <subject> <message>` posts a new one on the spot. A handful of boards need real rank to use -- try anyway and it''ll tell you. If a room happens to have more than one board, name which: `read at <board name>` / `write at <board name> <subject> <message>`.'),
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Much Sicker World', 'Diseases went from a short list to the whole thing -- 26 in all, from a common Cold up through Gangrene and the Plague, each with its own bite and its own price to cure. Poison got an upgrade too: drinking something foul now leaves you genuinely poisoned for a while (check `affects`), not just a single jolt of pain that vanished without a trace -- and yes, a hospital can cure that too now. NPCs can carry these same afflictions as players can; a diseased or poisoned creature nearby will visibly wince from it same as anyone else.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`look` Can Now Count Too', '`kill 2.mob` and `get 2.sword` have worked for a while when more than one thing matches -- `look` was the odd one out. Now it counts the same way: `look board` looks at the first matching thing in the room (or your own gear), `look 2.board` the second, and so on. Same trick works for `drink`/`sip` (a room with two puddles), `open` (two chests), `show`, `sell`, and reading/writing to a specific bulletin board when a room has more than one.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Few Bugs Squashed', '`stat obj/mob/room <name>` now actually searches by name when you don''t give it a vnum, instead of quietly showing you whatever happens to sit at vnum 0. The doctor''s hospital greeting is properly capitalized now ("The Tobin City Doctor...", not "the..."). And `load` (Immortal-only) now warns you -- without ever refusing -- if spawning something pushes its world count past what that prototype was ever meant to have loose at once, so it''s easier to remember to clean up after testing.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Torches and Lanterns Actually Work Now', '`light`/`extinguish <item> [held|room]` turn a light source on or off; `refuel <light> <fuel> [held|room]` tops one up from a fuel brick (Lumor''s Illuminations sells them). A lit light burns down over time and eventually goes out on its own -- refuel it before then. You can''t refuel while it''s lit (might explode), and a torch can''t be refueled at all, only replaced. A lit light source shows "(lit)" where it''s sitting so you can actually tell.'),
('The TobinMUD Team', 'The Lamp-Lighting Boy Is Back on the Job', 'Grimhaven''s lamp-lighting boy (and Brightmoon''s eager page) once again light the streetlamps at dusk and put them out at dawn, wherever they happen to be standing when it happens -- an honest, much smaller version of the original''s scripted patrol route, not the real thing.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'A Few Small Ones', '`prompt gold` joins `prompt hp` -- turn on either or both. `point` (with no target, or aimed at someone) now mentions whatever you''re holding in your primary hand instead of always "pointing around randomly." And `toggle pk` is here: player-vs-player combat only ever works if BOTH sides have opted in with it -- otherwise `attack`/`kill`/`hit` simply can''t find the other player at all. Off by default; mobs are unaffected either way.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Tips Have Arrived', '`tips` shows a random gameplay tip on demand; anyone still on the newbie channel also gets one echoed automatically every so often. `tipedit <text>` / `tipedit list` / `tipedit delete <id>` (53+) manage the pool -- DB-backed like news and help, but kept flat like `bug`/`idea` since a tip is one short sentence, not long-form content.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`cast`/`pray` Grow Real Effects', 'Beyond heal/damage/sanctuary (the original v1), `cast`/`pray` now reuse this session''s own disease/poison/affect work: "cure poison"/"cure disease" genuinely cure (self or, for `pray`, a named target); a wide family of armor/shield/resistance-flavored spells (stone skin, barkskin, self-wards, armor, bless, plasma mirror, ...) all apply the same Sanctuary ward "sanctuary" itself already used; and a Cleric fighting someone can now `pray poison`/`pray disease`/`pray infect` to actually inflict them, mirroring the pre-existing damage branch''s targeting and messaging. Scope-down, same as before: no mana (no pool exists), no elemental-resistance/damage-type model, and teleport/summon/polymorph/invisibility-flavored spells are untouched -- still "nothing happens yet." `skills`/`practice <discipline>` now open each Mage/Druid/Cleric section with a line naming exactly what `cast`/`pray` needs on hand for everything in it.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Every Skill and Spell Now Has a Real Help File', 'Correction to the entry above: the `skills`/`practice` inline reagent note was NOT a substitute for individual help files, as originally (wrongly) scoped -- user: "also no spell helpfiles? that was my instruction. help files for all skills/spells along with required components." Redone properly: `help <skill or spell name>` now works for all 272 entries in skill.c''s roster (generated straight from the SKILLS[] array itself, so it can''t hand-transcription-drift), each naming its class(es)/tier/level and what `cast`/`pray` needs -- a component, a symbol, the real command it already routes through (Thief''s door traps), or an honest "not yet wired to a command" for the many still-placeholder physical skills. New `Requires:` footer line (cmd_help.c, generalized from the existing `Related:` trailing-directive convention) renders this above Related, as asked. Also fixed along the way: `help <topic>` previously only ever looked up the FIRST WORD of a multi-word query, so "help cure poison" silently landed on "cure blindness" (alphabetically first among several same-prefixed "cure ___" topics) -- now tries the full argument as an exact/prefix match first. `disease`''s pre-existing topic got a content refresh too (26 diseases, not the original 4, plus the Cleric prayer that inflicts one).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`engage` Joins `hit`', 'A full alias for `hit` (real multi-round combat, never an instakill even for immortals) -- same one-handler-two-table-rows pattern `attack`/`kill` already use.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Skill/Spell Levels Rescaled to a 1-25-50 Curve', 'Real problem, not cosmetic: dozens of skill.c entries had a min_level ABOVE 50 (up to 99) -- since IMMORTAL_LEVEL_MIN is 51, those were permanently unreachable by any mortal, ever. Rescaled the whole roster with a one-shot script: Combat and Class tiers now span 1-25 per class, Advanced spans 25-50, each class rescaled independently off its own prior min/max (relative order preserved, no reshuffling of which skill unlocks before which within a class). skill_help.sql regenerated to match -- caught a real bug in the process, its own ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE name=name no-op (there to protect hedit edits from a reseed) meant simply reapplying the file after regenerating content silently changed nothing; worked around with an explicit delete-then-reapply for this one-time need. Also fixed two pre-existing test gaps this exposed: an un-paged `skills` read in smoke_test_immortal_castpray.py, and a missing combat_disc_pct in smoke_test_affects.py''s Cleric fixture (unrelated to the rescale, just never noticed until now).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Components Have Charges, Symbols Genuinely Decay', 'User: "how long does each component last? should be getting 10 casts out of each component and the symbols should decay as in sneezy." Both were single-use before, always, regardless. Researched the real source (obj_component.h/.cc, discipline.cc''s requireHolySym()) rather than guessing: the original''s TComponent really does have a charges counter, and TSymbol really does have a decaying strength pool that can shatter mid-prayer. Ported the SHAPE, not the exact numbers (the original''s decay scales off a per-symbol level rating Tobin symbols don''t have): obj.h''s val0/val1 now hold current/max charges (components) or current/max strength (symbols). A component spends exactly 1 charge per cast attempt, destroyed only once the last one''s gone. A symbol loses a random 1-2 strength per prayer/continue attempt, shattering once it runs out -- real variance, not a clean countdown. tobin_migrations.sql seeds every real component/symbol to 10/10, unconditionally -- several symbol rows had huge (up to 1.8 million) leftover val0/val1 from the upstream import that would have flatly contradicted "10 casts" if inherited as-is. New tests/smoke_test_component_charges.py covers both live.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'News Follow-Ups: Edit/Delete In-Game, Unseen-News Notice', 'User 2026-07-17 batch: "edit/delete existing news in-game (addnews only creates); show unseen news at login (per-player last-seen)." `edit news <existing headline>` now preloads the current body ("existing text below", same convention `edit help` already uses) and overwrites it in place on save -- `news_repo_upsert()` replaces the old straight INSERT that just failed on the duplicate title. `edit news delete <headline>` removes an item outright. `edit wiznews` got the identical treatment (same underlying gap, same shared EDIT_NEWS/EDIT_WIZNEWS save path in descriptor.c). New `player.news_last_seen_id` column (tobin_migrations.sql) bookmarks the highest news.id each player has caught up on -- bumped whenever they run `news`, checked at login to show a one-line "There is new news! Type ''news'' to catch up." notice (never a count or id -- house rule stands). tests/smoke_test_news_followups.py.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Look/Prompt: Three Stray Blank Lines Gone', 'User: found three separate extra \\r\\n in everyday output while testing the rebuilt box. (1) `look`''s room description had a spurious blank line before Exits -- root cause was DATA, not the format string: ~95%% of seeded room.description rows carry their own trailing "\\n" (upstream SneezyMUD convention), which stacked with cmd_look.c''s own appended \\r\\n. Fixed by trimming a COPY of the description before rendering (the stored row itself is untouched, so redit/stat/etc. still see the raw text). (2) The blank line cmd_look.c inserted between the Exits line and the room-contents listing is gone -- contents now follow Exits directly. (3) The per-turn game prompt (game_loop.c) was sending "\\r\\n\\r\\n" before "HP: ... > " in both the flags-on and flags-off branches -- double what the original comment says was intended ("insert A \\r\\n before each new prompt"); both now send a single \\r\\n. Verified against tests/smoke_test_exits_display.py, smoke_test_look_capitalization.py, smoke_test_look_equipment.py, smoke_test_look_fixture_order.py, smoke_test_room_stacking.py, smoke_test_parser_display.py -- all still pass.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Thief `peek` Skill: See What Someone Is Carrying', 'User: "a thief skill could be added to attempt a peak at the targets inventory." New `peek <target>` shows a target''s loose CARRIED inventory (not worn -- `look <target>` already covers that) if you know the skill and the roll succeeds; a fumble tips the target off, a clean success stays silent -- same "attempt made, effect not guaranteed" shape as trap mechanics (cmd_trap.c), which this was modeled directly on. New "peek" entry in skill.c (Thief, Combat tier, level 1, right after "steal"). Placed in cmd_table.c right after `pee`, not alphabetically near pray/practice, so an immortal typing bare "pee" keeps meaning the pee command -- "peek" shares its first three letters and cmd_dispatch() resolves a shared abbreviation to whichever entry comes first. Caught a genuinely useful gate detail writing the smoke test: `being_knows_skill()` requires `combat_disc_pct > 0` for ANY Combat-tier skill, not just the level threshold -- a fresh level-1 character can''t use ANY Combat skill (peek included) until they''ve practiced at least once. tests/smoke_test_peek.py.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Player-State Logging: pfile Changes Now Searchable', 'get/drop were already logged (LOG_SILENT, cmd_object.c) -- this closes the other half of the old TODO item ("log get/drop + pfile changes so `log search <name>` tells a player''s story"): `title`, `prompt`, `poofin`/`poofout`, and `bamfin`/`bamfout` each now log a LOG_SILENT line on set/clear, same convention as get/drop. `color` was deliberately left out -- it lives on `account`, not `player`, and gets toggled far more casually (a terminal preference, not a piece of a player''s story). tests/smoke_test_pfile_logging.py.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Trigger Scripts Get `say` and a Real `wait`', 'User pasted a Monty-Python-style market-vendor script ("wait 1 / say Larks'' tongues. / wait 1 / say Wrens'' livers. / ...") and asked to put it on a mob. Neither verb existed -- the vocabulary was echo/echoroom/emote/teleport/give/damage/log, and a script ran start-to-finish synchronously in one pass, no pause primitive at all. Confirmed the direction with the user first (this genuinely expands the trigger system past its own documented "deliberately small" design intent, trigger.sql) before building. `say <text>` renders "<Name> says, ''<text>''" to the room. `wait <seconds>` (1-3600) pauses everything after that line and schedules a real continuation (new trigger_pending_tick(), ~1s pulse cadence) -- the triggering actor is deliberately NOT carried across the pause (may be long gone by the time it resumes); only room/self are, safely re-derived fresh from the trigger''s own target_type/target_vnum rather than holding a raw pointer across an open-ended real-time gap. `aitick` now forces pending waits along too, for deterministic testing. Caught two real bugs along the way: the new pulse registration silently exceeded a fixed MAX_PULSE_PROCESSES limit and got dropped (wait would have just never fired -- bumped 16 to 24), and aitick''s original step ordering could resolve a wait in the very same pass that scheduled it, collapsing the pause into a no-op. tests/smoke_test_trigger_wait.py.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Zone Fix: 1119 Mobs That Were Never Actually Spawning', 'Chasing why a trigger on mob 149 never fired for a real player led somewhere much bigger: the mob was never spawning through the normal zone reset system at all, in ANY zone. Root-caused against the real SneezyMUD C++ source: the upstream zonefile opcode `A <lo> <hi>` rolls a random room number in that range and stores it as the zone''s current "random room" (the original engine''s ZONE_ROOM_RANDOM = -99); any following M/O command whose own room argument is that same -99 sentinel uses the rolled room instead of a literal vnum. Tobin''s zone engine only ever implemented six opcodes (M/O/E/G/P/D) -- `A` was never one of them, so its -99 sentinel was never substituted with anything and always failed to resolve. Confirmed via direct query: 1119 mob-load rows and 25 object-load rows across the whole database use -99 as their room -- all wandering/ambient content by theme (grimhaven youth, scarred tomcat, filthy dog, ugly crow, ...), never meant to sit in one fixed spot, exactly matching what this opcode is for. New zone_cmd_random_room()/zone_resolve_target_room() (src/core/zone.c) close the gap. Verified live: preview''s boot mob/object count jumped from 649/587 to 769/652 with the fix in, and mob 149''s own two -99 rolls landed in different real rooms across repeated resets, as designed.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', '`scan` Range Trimmed', 'User: "scan works too well, can we cut the range down to 4 or 5 rooms distance?" SCAN_MAX_RANGE dropped from 6 to 5 (cmd_scan.c) -- one fewer "way off in the distance" tier.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Zone Reset: World-Wide max_exist Now Actually Enforced', 'User: "i used scan and got 50-60 mobs listed." A real regression the same-day A-opcode fix exposed: a ZONE_ROOM_RANDOM (-99) mob lands in a different room every reset, so the existing per-room cap (is THIS room already full of this vnum) was trivially satisfied every time -- confirmed live, zone 100''s periodic reset alone was adding ~190 fresh mob instances on top of whatever already existed, on every single firing. The original engine gates this with a world-wide instance-count check; ported as zone_world_count() plus a check in zone_cmd_load_mob()/zone_cmd_load_obj_ground() before creating anything, refusing over cap rather than just warning (unlike the immortal `load` command''s existing warn-only version). Verified live: forced zone 100 to reset 3 times running, then scanned its full random-room range room by room -- mob 149 (its own cap is 8) sat at exactly 8 live instances, not climbing. A production restart was needed alongside this fix, not instead of it -- nothing persists live mob/object state, so a fresh boot is what actually clears out the bloat the running process had already built up; the code fix is what stops it recurring.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Keys Unlocking Doors -- and Containers', 'TODO.md''s "Keys unlocking doors" item, unblocked now that the object system exists. New `lock`/`unlock <direction>` and `lock`/`unlock <container>` (cmd_lock.c). Matching rule confirmed against the real SneezyMUD C++ source (has_key()/keyCheck(), misc/movement.cc) before writing a line of code: a key is identified by its OWN object vnum, NOT by any val[] field on the key itself -- a door''s `roomexit.key_num` (loaded and silently discarded until now -- room_t gained an `exit_key[]` field to actually carry it) or a container''s `val[2]` names the vnum a carried object must have. Both must be closed first before `lock` will touch them; `unlock` only needs the key. Real, immediately testable seeded data: 1,141 doors and dozens of containers across the live DB already carry working key_num/val[2] values that were simply inert until this. Also fixed two stale comments left over from before this landed (obj.h''s KEY val[0] doc was flatly wrong -- val[] is unused, matching-by-vnum confirmed against real seeded key rows; cmd_open.c''s "deferred" note). New tests/smoke_test_keys.py (23 checks: keyhole-less doors, no/wrong/right key on both a door and a container, lock-requires-closed, persistence to roomexit.condition_flag). Table entries verified with tests/tools/cmd_abbrev_check.py (also patched -- it was missing three level macros added by later sessions and errored out before this).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Mid-Fight HP Now Survives a Disconnect', 'TODO.md''s "Mid-fight persistence" item. HP was previously only saved at defeat/quit (`descriptor_leave_to_menu()`) -- a mid-fight disconnect (crash, or a losing player quietly pulling the plug) reloaded at whatever HP was last saved BEFORE the fight even started, silently undoing all damage taken. `combat_process_run()` (combat.c) now calls the existing `player_progress_save()` for both PC participants after every round the fight is still ongoing, not just at defeat -- the exact same call the defeat/gold-drop paths already made, no new persistence mechanism invented. Limb HP is explicitly NOT covered -- it isn''t persisted at all yet by ANY path, defeat included; that remains the separate "Meaningful limb damage" item. Verified with a real crash simulation, not just a code read: new tests/smoke_test_mid_fight_persist.py fights a deliberately tanky sandbox mob (2025 HP so it survives many rounds without dying, weak enough not to threaten the 300-HP test PC) until real damage lands, then hard-closes the socket with no `quit!` and reconnects -- HP landed exactly where it was the instant before the disconnect (298/300 in the observed run), not reset to the pre-fight max.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Split Gold on Kill -- Solo Case', 'TODO.md''s "Split victim''s gold among the group on kill" item, solo half. User: "upon death get all gold from the victim and split it between all group members if groupped." `combat_defeat()` (combat.c): a PC loser''s entire wallet transfers to a non-immortal PC winner, right alongside the existing HP-reset-to-half/limb-heal defeat handling -- reuses the loser''s existing `player_progress_save()` call plus the winner''s existing XP-block save (gold and XP land on the same struct before that save fires, so no new DB write was added for this). Same non-immortal-winner gate as the pre-existing mob-gold-drop path, so an immortal''s instakill never triggers it; PK combat already requires both sides to have opted in (`toggle pk`), so there is no non-consensual path here. The "if grouped" split half remains genuinely blocked on the not-yet-built group/party system -- this lands as the natural insertion point for it once that exists. New tests/smoke_test_pk_gold.py (a 1-HP loser so the very first landed hit resolves the fight deterministically).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Meaningful Limb Damage -- Auditing What Already Shipped', 'TODO.md''s "Meaningful limb damage" item. User: "make limb damage mean something. if you have a limb decapitated it shouldnt be at 100% limb health. make individual limb hits actually hurt." Turned out most of this already existed from earlier sessions and just was never checked off: per-limb hit weighting, escalating injury-tier messages (combat + score), a destroyed limb already penalizing its OWNER''s own hit chance, and Hospital already giving a real mid-game cure. The genuine gap: `combat_strike()` only ever checked the ATTACKER''s destroyed limb -- a maimed defender wasn''t any easier to hit, only worse at fighting back. Added the mirror check (combat.c), same flat non-stacking amount. On the "still shows 100%" half: a destroyed limb DOES correctly read 0% while a fight is ongoing -- it only heals back after the fight ends because combat defeat has always fully healed every limb as part of its "revived at half HP" recovery, same as HP itself. That''s deliberate soft-respawn design (PC death isn''t permadeath here), not a bug -- documented explicitly in being.h''s `being_has_destroyed_limb()` doc comment and combat.c so it doesn''t get mistaken for one again. Also corrected a stale comment in the same spot claiming "no hospital system yet" -- Hospital shipped 2026-07-18.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Ignore Lists', 'First item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit''s remaining-work list (user audited the published artifact and asked to work through the rest). Checked Sneezy''s own communication-system.md doc before building anything: the original''s ignoreList blocks by descriptor/name/whole-account across say/tell/whisper/shout/grouptell/emote/socials, silent-to-sender failure. Scoped to what Tobin actually has: a flat per-character name list (new `player_ignore` table, `ignore_repo.h`/`.c`), checked only by `tell`/`whisper` -- the broadcast channels (say/socials) would need a per-listener filter loop at every room-echo call site for comparatively little value, since those aren''t where unwanted DMs actually come from. `ignore [<name>]`/`unignore <name>` (cmd_ignore.c), up to 30 entries. A blocked sender''s tell/whisper still reports success on their own screen, matching the original''s documented behavior exactly. New tests/smoke_test_ignore.py (13 checks).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Possess/Return: Puppet a Mob''s Body', 'Second item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list ("Switch / return"). Checked Sneezy''s own 23-snoop-switch.md doc first: admin switch is a raw descriptor-pointer swap, no stat transfer, no transformation message -- that''s the separate spell-driven polymorph flavor (its own, still-open "Transformation" audit item). Named `possess` since `switch` already means something else in Tobin (swap held items). `possess <mob>` (59+)/`return` (cmd_possess.c) -- new `descriptor_t.possess_original` holds the immortal''s own PC while `character` points at the puppet; the original goes temporarily desc==NULL, the exact same shape a real link-drop already leaves a body in. Two real bugs caught testing this, not just guessed from the doc: cmd_dispatch()''s level gate was reading the PUPPET''s seeded level (often 1), which would have made `return` itself invisible and permanently stranded an immortal inside whatever they possessed -- fixed by gating on the real immortal''s level while possessing. And a disconnect mid-possession was about to run the link-drop announce/log/parking against the mob instead of the immortal -- fixed by restoring the real character first, at the top of descriptor_destroy(). `quit!` while possessing is refused outright (return first). New tests/smoke_test_possess.py (9 checks, including recovery from a hard disconnect mid-possession).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'wiznet @<level> Targeting', 'Third item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list ("OOC channels"). Checked Sneezy''s own communication-system.md doc: `wiznet` already covered the real doCommune()''s default behavior (every online immortal, sender included); the one documented gap was the `@<level>` prefix that narrows delivery to gods at or above that level. Added the equivalent (`wiznet @<level> <message>`, composes with the `;` shorthand). Deliberately did NOT add a separate "commune" command -- that would just duplicate wiznet; the real gap was the targeting feature. New tests/smoke_test_wiznet.py (4 checks).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Group / Party System', 'Fourth item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list. Checked Sneezy''s own 09-group-party.md doc first, then scoped it down deliberately (documented inline in being.h): no leader-succession algorithm (a group just dissolves if the leader leaves/dies, not the original''s two-pass reformGroup()); no per-player configurable 1-10 `group share` money factor -- gold splits EVENLY instead; XP shares are level-weighted, a simplification of the original''s mob_exp()-based share; no quest-flag or charm/mount interaction checks; `follow` only refuses a direct circular pair, not a full chain walk. New `master`/`grouped`/`followers[]` fields on being_t -- live in-memory only, same rule as `fighting`, except a raw disconnect deliberately does NOT clear them so a group survives a member briefly dropping link, matching the original. `follow <name>`/`stop`/`group [<name>|all]` (leader-only to add)/`split <amount>` (leader-only, divides evenly, refuses a share that would round to 0) -- cmd_group.c. combat.c''s new group_recipients() helper feeds the existing XP-award, PK gold-steal, and mob-gold-drop blocks so a grouped, in-room, non-immortal winner''s kill benefits the whole party, while the exact solo-winner math/messages stay untouched when nobody''s grouped (regression-verified clean against the existing PK-gold and level-up-HP smoke tests). Two real test-infrastructure lessons hit along the way: a direct SQL update on an already-connected character isn''t seen in memory without a relog, and `quit!` itself saves the player -- so a direct SQL update has to run AFTER the quit, not before, or the save silently clobbers it. New tests/smoke_test_group.py (14 checks across all 6 phases).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Hunger, Thirst, and Age', 'Fifth item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list. Checked Sneezy''s own vital-statistics.md doc first, then scoped it down hard: the original''s cryptic 0-24 FULL/THIRST units become a plain 0-100 percent; PEE/POOP/DRUNK conditions are out of scope; drain is a flat rate, not the original''s terrain-weighted two-stage-random roll (terrain factors belong to the still-open "Terrain movement cost" item). Age (user, AskUserQuestion 2026-07-19): track + display only, explicitly not the original''s full graf()-interpolated age-based stat-curve system across 6 stats with a vampire exemption -- real machinery for a mostly-cosmetic payoff here. New progress_t.hunger/thirst (-1 = immortal-immune) and .birth_time. Drains 1 hunger + 1 thirst every ~60s tick; starvation (hunger OR thirst at 0) costs 1 HP per tick, floored at 1 -- same "never lethal outside real combat" precedent cmd_sip.c''s poison roll already set, since real starvation death waits on the still-open "Death processing" item. New `eat <food>` restores hunger by the food object''s own val0 (already well-populated in the real seed, 1-24) and fully consumes it in one bite, sidestepping a real upstream data gap (every seeded FOOD''s val1 "current units" is uniformly 0) instead of migrating around it. `drink`/`sip` now also raise thirst -- a fountain fully refills it, a puddle or sip only partly. `score` shows Hunger/Thirst as words and a real-elapsed-time Age line; immortals see "Immune". `aitick` now also forces a vitals tick, so tests don''t wait on real time. New tests/smoke_test_vitals.py (13 checks).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'XP Loss on Death', 'Sixth item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list ("Death processing"). Checked Sneezy''s own death-processing.md doc first: real death there is a whole permadeath pipeline (die() -> rawKill() -> makeCorpse()) with a corpse and a resurrection spell to revive it. Asked before building anything (AskUserQuestion) since Tobin''s PC death was already NOT permadeath -- combat_defeat() already half-heals HP, fully heals limbs, and ejects the loser to the account menu, same soft-respawn design as before this session. Answer: XP loss only -- "resurrection" is already covered by the existing relog flow, so no corpse/spell system was built. combat_defeat() (combat.c) now docks XP on a PC''s death: min(20% of current XP, XP banked past the current level''s own threshold) -- adapted from Sneezy''s own min(20%, 25*mob_exp(level)), but the cap here needs no separate mob-XP curve (just the existing progress_xp_for_level()) and guarantees a death can never de-level anyone. A PvP death (mutual `toggle pk`) divides the result by 10; a death to a mob gets the full penalty. Immortals never lose XP. New tests/smoke_test_death_xploss.py (6 checks: full 20% loss with ample banked XP, the de-level-safety cap kicking in with only a little banked, and the PvP 1/10th reduction) -- regression-verified against smoke_test_pk_gold.py, smoke_test_levelup_hp.py, and smoke_test_group.py, which all also exercise combat_defeat().')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Junk and Identify', 'Seventh item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list ("Object manipulation depth"). `sacrifice` was skipped after asking (AskUserQuestion) -- it''s entirely a Shaman-class skill tied to a `lifeforce` resource and totems in the original, and Tobin has none of those, so there was no honest way to keep the verb without inventing a whole system nothing else needs. `junk <item>` is a straight port of the original''s own command (not skill-gated there either): destroys a carried, loose item for good, same scope as `drop`. `identify <item>` is new, a plain command (not a spell, same tier as `examine`) showing a carried item''s real category-specific stats. Building it surfaced two real mismatches between obj.h''s val[] doc comment and reality, fixed by showing only what''s mechanically real: a weapon''s val0/val1 are NOT damage dice despite the doc comment (verified against the real seed -- one dart carries val0=4626, val1=2073, nonsense as dice -- and against combat.c''s actual formula, which never reads either field), so identify shows the real hitroll/damroll bonus instead; and a magic item''s val0 "charges" is likewise unreliable (5 on potions, a nonsense 25650 on one scroll) and unread by any code yet (no use/zap/quaff command exists), so identify says so honestly instead of printing a possibly-garbage number. Also found and fixed a real latent bug: `eat` (previous audit item) destroyed the eaten food but never saved the resulting inventory change, so a reconnect before any other inventory-touching action would resurrect the "eaten" item from its still-persisted DB row -- `junk` would have had the identical gap. Both now save immediately after destroying, matching the pattern cmd_shop.c''s sell path already used correctly. New tests/smoke_test_objmanip.py (10 checks, including a regression check for the resurrection bug on both commands).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Quest System (Infrastructure)', 'Eighth item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list. Checked Sneezy''s own quest-system.md doc first: the real system is a fixed 454-bit array tied entirely to hand-authored content that doesn''t exist here -- specific quests like "Avenger"/"Silverclaw"/"Holy Devastator", named NPCs, dialogue trees driven by spec procedures. Porting the bit array itself would just be 454 meaningless numbers with nothing behind them, and Tobin''s trigger-script language has no conditional branching yet to drive quest logic even with new storage in place. Asked the user (AskUserQuestion) rather than inventing bespoke quest content or a whole scripting layer; answer was infrastructure only. Ported the SHAPE of the original instead of its bit numbers: a player''s stage in a named quest, visible only where an immortal has written a description for that exact stage -- same "only bits with a help file are visible" rule, DB rows instead of files. New `quest [<name>]` (mortal): bare form lists quests with a written description for the player''s current stage; `quest <name>` shows the full description. `questdef <name> <stage> <text>` (builder tier) writes/replaces a stage''s description. Advancing a player''s stage is a new `quest` field on the existing `set <name> <field> <value>` -- manual, since there''s no scripting hook yet to automate it. New tests/smoke_test_quest.py (9 checks).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Weather, and Darkness That Actually Matters', 'Ninth item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list. Checked Sneezy''s own Weather class first: a real barometric-pressure random walk, a moon-phase cycle, per-room wetness, season-aware sunrise/sunset -- trimmed to a single world-wide sky state (clear/cloudy/rainy/stormy) advanced by a simple weighted table instead of a pressure simulation. New `weather` command shows the current condition, a flavor hint (not a real forecast), and day/night; changes announce world-wide, same precedent gametime.c''s own noon/midnight lines already use. The real payoff is the other half: gametime_is_daytime() already existed (the lamplighter mob used it) but nothing gated visibility on it until now. A room is dark for anyone without an active light source unless it''s flagged ALWAYS-LIT/INDOORS or it''s daytime -- wired into both `look` and `exits`, which is what finally makes carried light sources matter. Building this surfaced a real cross-test hazard: since darkness is brand new, any existing test with a plain unflagged sandbox room that calls look/exits on it can now intermittently fail depending on the server''s current in-game time of day. One real casualty found and fixed (smoke_test_look_equipment.py); a broader audit of the rest of the suite was spun off as a follow-up rather than expanding this session further. New tests/smoke_test_weather.py (8 checks) -- it also restores a neutral daytime hour when it finishes, so it doesn''t leave the shared server clock stuck at night for whatever test runs next.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Extra Descriptions: look <keyword> Now Reveals Hidden Room Detail', 'Follow-up work found while auditing the darkness gate for cross-test hazards (previous entry): TODO.md''s "redit Extra Descriptions" item noted the upstream `roomextra` table already existed with real content, but nothing in Tobin actually read it -- 8,861 seeded rows (room vnum, a space-separated keyword list, a description) sitting completely inert. `look <keyword>` (cmd_look.c''s look_at_target()) now checks a room''s roomextra rows after a real PC/mob/object match misses, same case-insensitive per-word prefix matching every other keyword lookup in this codebase already uses. Verified live against real content, not just the sandbox test -- room 3''s seeded "calendar" extra desc ("Grumble car payment due on the 25th") comes back correctly. New tests/smoke_test_extra_desc.py (5 checks). Still open: the BUILDER-facing half -- an in-game `redit` list/add/edit/delete for authoring new ones, rather than direct SQL.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Aggressive Mobs Now Give Chase', 'Tenth item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list ("Monster AI & behavior (pursuit)"). Checked Sneezy''s own 14-monster-ai-behavior.md doc first: the real system is a huge opinion/emotion model (suspicion/greed/malice/anger, per-mob hate and fear memory, faction territorial combat) plus a real multi-room hunt() with persistence counters and pathfinding -- none of that exists in Tobin, and building it wholesale was way out of scope for one audit item. Scoped "(pursuit)" down to exactly what it names: an ACT_AGGRESSIVE mob a player successfully flees from now gets one immediate, single-room, 50% chance to follow into the next room and resume the fight, before the fleeing player''s own `look` renders -- so a successful chase is already standing there in the room description, not a surprise arrival after the fact. No persistence, no cross-tick hunting state, no real multi-room hunt; a PC opponent from PK combat never gives chase this way. New tests/smoke_test_pursuit.py (2 checks: an aggressive mob eventually chases across repeated flee attempts; a non-aggressive mob never does) -- verified the underlying dice are genuinely random, not accidentally deterministic, by re-running it 5 times and confirming real variance in how many attempts it took (1, 1, 1, 3, 4). Regression-checked against smoke_test_mob_ai.py and smoke_test_group.py, which also exercise mob AI and combat.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Vitality: Walking Costs Something Now', 'Eleventh item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list ("Vitality stat + Terrain movement cost"). Vitality doesn''t exist in the original either -- its "moves" resource (misc/limits.cc''s getMaxMove()/moveGain()) is CON-and-level-derived but otherwise unnamed; Vitality is a Tobin-original name closing the "Depends on Vitality" TODO.md fragment this item''s own audit note pointed at. New `vit`/`max_vit` on every character (progress_t, being.h) -- 50 base + CON bonus + 2/level, shown in `score` and now toggleable into the prompt (`prompt vit`, previously blocked alongside mana/piety), regenerates on the same weight-by-position tick as HP (regen.c). Terrain cost: the original''s TerrainInfo[61] gives every sector its own hand-tuned movement cost; Tobin has no per-sector content to justify that granularity, so `sector_move_cost()` (room.c) buckets all 61 sectors into 6 cost tiers by name (roads/cities cheapest, lava/solid rock/fire priciest), reusing the same substring-grouping precedent `sector_color()` already established. `north`/`east`/etc (cmd_move.c) now charge the average of the source and destination sector''s cost, straight out of the average-of-two-sectors rule -- refusing the move outright ("You are too exhausted to go that way") rather than letting vit go negative, same hard-gate shape as the door/fighting checks already there. No swim/fly/mount modifiers yet -- those stay blocked on the still-open "Water, drowning, flight" and "Mount / riding" items; water sectors are just an expensive-but-walkable tier for now. Immortals are exempt, same precedent as hunger/thirst immunity. New tests/smoke_test_vitality_terrain.py (10 checks) -- building it surfaced a real timing gotcha worth knowing about: regen_tick_run() heals vit roughly every 5 real seconds but ONLY in memory (it never itself saves to the DB), so a naive DB-read-based test can flake against a stray regen tick a live `score` read doesn''t. Also fixed a stale, unrelated assertion in smoke_test_parser_display.py (its own "qu should NOT reach quit" check predated the `quest` command and needed updating to match "qu" now legitimately reaching quest instead).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Drowning Is Now Real -- And Lethal', 'Twelfth item off the Sneezy → Tobin feature audit list ("Water, drowning, flight"). Checked Sneezy''s own movement-terrain-navigation doc first: AFF_SWIM discounts water-sector movement cost, a procCharDrowning scheduler deals 1d10 every 3.6 real seconds to anyone underwater without AFF_WATERBREATH -- genuinely lethal -- and AFF_FLYING quarters movement cost and bypasses drowning entirely. Two Mage spells that were already listed but unimplemented now do something real: `cast gills of flesh` (level 9) grants water breathing, `cast levitate` (level 11) grants flight (both new AFFECT_ types, same shape as the existing Sanctuary ward -- check `affects` to see remaining duration). A new `sector_is_underwater()` distinguishes true UNDERWATER sectors from merely-wet surface water (ocean/river surface/iceflow) -- only the former drowns you. The drowning roll itself is the SAME 1d10 the original uses, just on Tobin''s existing ~60s vitals tick instead of the original''s 3.6s one, so it''s already far gentler in practice. Per AskUserQuestion with the user: unlike hunger/thirst/poison''s non-lethal floor-at-1 convention, drowning can genuinely kill -- same soft-respawn handling (half HP, healed limbs, XP loss, corpse, back to the account menu) as a real combat death, just with no killer to credit. New tests/smoke_test_water_drowning_flight.py (13 checks) covers all three pieces, including confirming an unprotected mortal actually drowns within a bounded number of forced ticks.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: wiznews (and news) Silently Cutting Off Mid-Sentence', 'User report, fixed same day given the severity ("wiznews bug with the pager/long output that freezes the mud"). Real bug, not a false alarm: `descriptor_page_start()`''s pager copied its whole source text into a fixed 16KB buffer before doling it out a page at a time -- anything longer than that got silently TRUNCATED, no warning, often mid-word. Both `wiznews` and the public `news` channel build up to 101KB of real content (a *previous* fix for the exact same class of overflow at an even smaller size) -- but that fix never reached the pager''s own buffer, so once either feed grew past ~16KB (both have, after everything posted this session alone) reading through it would just abruptly stop and dump you back at a normal prompt with the rest of the feed gone, no error, nothing to indicate it was cut off. That''s not a true infinite hang, but it is exactly the kind of "something''s clearly broken and stuck" experience that reads as the mud freezing. Fixed by growing the pager''s buffer to comfortably clear both feeds'' size with real headroom for future growth. Affects any command that pages long output, not just wiznews -- `news`/`help`/`who`/`skills` all share the same pager and all benefit from the same fix.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: Weather Was Leaking Indoors', 'User report: "weather should not affect rooms that are flagged indoors." Real bug -- the weather-change announcement ("Clouds begin to gather overhead", "It begins to rain", ...) was broadcast to every connected character regardless of what room they were standing in, so someone tucked away inside a building would still hear about clouds gathering overhead. Fixed with the exact same INDOORS-flag check the darkness half of the weather system already uses for `look`/`exits` -- a room flagged indoors simply does not receive the announcement anymore. A torchlit indoor room still gets the exemption (it just is not dark, that is a separate question from being indoors), matching how ALWAYS-LIT and INDOORS have always meant two different things everywhere else in the game.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Tips Channel Now Has Its Own Toggle', 'User request: "tips channel should be a toggle to shut it off or turn it on again." Previously the only way to stop the periodic "Tip:" echoes was `toggle newbie`, which also dropped you off the newbie help channel entirely -- a much bigger side effect than just wanting quieter tips. Added a standalone `toggle tips` switch (on by default) that only silences the tip echoes, leaving the newbie channel itself untouched.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New: The `level` Command', 'User request: "a level command that will display when your due for a gain in level, You have X experience and need X experience to level." `level` now tells you exactly that -- current experience, and how much more you need to reach the next level. At the mortal cap (level 50) or as an immortal, it tells you why there is nothing more to grind toward instead of showing a nonsensical number.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: Animals No Longer Carry Gold', 'User: "animal races should not have wealth, that doesnt make sense." Killing a mundane real-world creature -- a rat, a bear, a bird, a snake, and everything else in that vein -- no longer hands you a purse of coins on the kill; that never made much sense for an ordinary animal to be carrying. Fantastical or sapient monsters (dragons, orcs, goblins, undead, demons, and so on) are unaffected -- still exactly as lootable as before. Experience earned from the kill is unaffected either way.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Prompt: exp, expneed, and prompt all', 'User request: expand the customizable prompt beyond hp/gold/vit. `prompt exp` shows your current experience, `prompt expneed` shows how much more you need to reach the next level (same math as the `level` command). New `prompt all` turns on every available stat at once instead of toggling them one at a time. Mana and piety are still not available -- those resources do not exist in the game yet.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Looking at Someone Now Shows Their Inventory (Immortals Only)', 'User request: "immortals can see inventory when looking at a mob or player and can also see the contents of any container they carry." `look <target>` now appends a carried-inventory section for immortal viewers, right after the equipment listing -- items already shown as worn are not duplicated. An open container among their carried items shows what is inside it too, one level deep; a closed one just shows "(closed)". Mortals looking at someone else see no change at all.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'redit Can Now Author Extra Descriptions', 'The builder half of extra descriptions is here. `edit room`''s menu 8 opens a new submenu: list what a room already has, add a new one (type its keywords, then write the description in the usual line editor), rename an existing one''s keywords, edit its description text, delete one, or wipe every extra description on the room at once. Unlike the rest of `edit room`, these changes save immediately -- there is no separate Save step for them. A keyword set that already exists on that room cannot be reused for a different entry; renaming into a collision is refused, cleanly, with nothing lost. Anything written this way shows up right away to players via `look <keyword>`, the read-only half that shipped last session.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Socials Moved to the Database, and the Full Original Set Is Back', 'The old hardcoded list of emotes has been replaced with a proper database table, and the complete original set has been ported over -- a huge jump from the small handful we had before. Every one of them was checked carefully against the original game''s files rather than guessed at, including which ones require standing (or at least sitting up) to use, and how each one addresses you, the other player, and onlookers by name or pronoun. A couple of long-standing gaps got fixed along the way: aiming an emote at yourself now gets its own proper reaction instead of just repeating the plain version, and the `socials` list -- being so much longer now -- pages through screen by screen instead of dumping everything at once. Still to come: an in-game editor for builders to add or tweak socials without touching the database directly. One heads-up from testing this live tonight: our usual way of restarting the server on this box briefly took production down twice while chasing down two small bugs the bigger test suite caught, and it dropped the one player who happened to be online at the time. We do not currently have a safe way to do a zero-downtime restart (a "copyover") on this box without someone''s own logged-in immortal doing it by hand -- worth setting up before the next deploy like this.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New: edsocial, an In-Game Editor for Socials', 'The second half of last night''s socials work is here -- `edit social [name]` (level 55+), the missing piece now that every social lives in the database instead of hardcoded. Leave off the name to see the whole list; typing an existing one opens its detail screen, or type `new` to create one from scratch. From there you can rewrite any of its eight message variants (what you see, what onlookers see, what a target sees, and the dedicated messages for targeting yourself), toggle whether the message is hidden from someone who can''t see the actor (a real field from the original game, though it does nothing yet -- we don''t have invisibility in yet), change the minimum position needed to use it, rename it, or delete it outright. Nothing here needs a Save step or a server restart -- every change takes effect immediately, live, the moment you make it. While building this, a completely unrelated but serious bug turned up and got fixed the same night: an immortal typing plain `skills` (the "show every class, everything unlocked" view) was silently crashing the server, because that view''s buffer was too small for the full skill list and the overflow wasn''t being caught -- see the separate write-up below.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Fixed: An Immortal Typing `skills` Was Crashing the Server', 'Found live, the hard way, mid-testing: any immortal running the plain `skills` command (the view that shows every class''s entire skill list at once, fully unlocked) reliably crashed the whole server. The list has grown to roughly three hundred entries across every class, and the buffer that view builds its output in was sized for a much smaller game -- once it filled up, the code kept writing past the end of it instead of stopping cleanly, which is what actually brought the server down rather than just cutting the list short. Fixed on both counts: the buffer is now sized with real headroom for how big the game has actually gotten, and every place that builds up this kind of list now refuses to write past the end no matter how much content there ends up being, so a full server crash from this class of bug shouldn''t be possible again anywhere else it''s used. Caught and confirmed via a debugger attached to the live process during testing, not guessed at from a vague symptom -- we''re now keeping a debugger attached by habit while testing on these boxes going forward, specifically so a crash like this gets an exact cause immediately instead of just looking like a mysterious wave of unrelated test failures.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New Combat Skills: bash, kick, disarm, and a Passive parry', 'Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit item. `bash` (Warrior), `kick` (Thief/Monk), and `disarm` (Warrior/Thief/Monk) are new mid-fight commands -- extra actions layered on top of the automatic per-round exchange, not a replacement for it -- each gated by a single skill_roll_success() roll (same learn-by-doing shape cast/pray/peek already use) and costing the attacker a lag-style wait regardless of outcome, matching the original''s addSkillLag() always firing win or lose. A successful bash also knocks the defender down and costs THEM a round; the original''s own real bash deals no direct damage at all ("should NOT cause loss of attacks, or do damage; only prevent skill-use"), a deliberate, disclosed deviation kept because a no-op success reads as broken rather than faithful. `parry` has no player command at all -- checked Sneezy''s own source first and its real `parry` command is a disabled stub (`doParry()` just prints it isn''t supported); the actual mechanic is entirely passive, wired directly into the core hit-resolution chain with no cooldown, so that''s exactly how it''s ported here too (Warrior only, proficiency quartered so full proficiency caps near a 25% block chance). New shared `combat_apply_skill_damage()` handles the "this damage might finish the fight, and if the loser is a mob it just got destroy()''d" hazard bash/kick share with the automatic combat path. New `tests/smoke_test_skillcombat.py` (12 checks).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Linkdead Bodies Now Auto-Purge After 5 Minutes', 'TODO.md priority item, found live: a linkdead PC''s in-memory body was staying fully resident in its room forever -- nothing ever cleaned one up except that same account reconnecting, a manual `purge linkdead`, or a process restart. Confirmed as a real, measurable server slowdown twice in one day, independently, on both boxes -- accumulated zombie characters (mostly from smoke tests that raw-close a socket instead of `quit`ting) bogging down the combat pulse loop for everyone. Checked the original game''s own `nukeLdead()` first: it force-saves a linkdead character before stripping/freeing them. Asked the user directly which behavior Tobin should match, rather than defaulting silently -- chose save-then-destroy, same as the original, over discard-only (which is what Tobin''s existing on-demand `purge linkdead` already does, and still does -- this is a separate, additional, automatic path). Flat 5 minutes for everyone here, not the original''s 15-minute-mortal/60-minute-immortal split. Runtime-configurable (`TOBIN_LINKDEAD_PURGE_SECONDS`) specifically so both halves -- the removal AND the save actually capturing current, not stale, state -- could be verified live with a shortened threshold rather than just trusting the build. One disclosed trade-off worth knowing: an admin DB edit made to a character that''s been linkdead 5+ minutes or more can still be overwritten by their stale pre-disconnect data at the moment of purge -- the same narrow risk the game''s normal link-drop handling already accepts for everyday disconnects, just now also reachable 5 minutes later by this new path.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Offensive Spells Got Real Breadth', 'Cast and pray''s attack spells had three quiet gaps since they first landed: every single one dealt the exact same flat amount of damage no matter which spell it was (a starting Mage''s weakest bolt hit exactly as hard as a level-50 caster''s strongest one), they could only ever be aimed at whoever you were already fighting -- there was no way to open a fight with a spell at all, and `cast` in particular had no way to name a target the way `pray` always could -- and a kill landed this way skipped the normal death handling entirely. All three are fixed. Damage now scales with the spell itself, not a flat number. `cast <spell> <target>` works now, and either command can open a brand-new fight with someone standing right there. And several spells that always claimed to be "area-effect" in their own description -- fireball, tsunami, hellfire, plague of locusts, earthquake, and others -- actually hit everyone else in the room now instead of quietly acting single-target the whole time, matching what they said on the label from day one. A private note for whoever picks up testing next: an early draft of this briefly introduced a real bug where a plain self-heal prayer would have healed your opponent instead of you if you were already mid-fight -- caught and fixed before it ever shipped, by re-checking the design rather than waiting for a test to catch it, but worth knowing the shape of the mistake in case anything like it turns up again.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Magic Items: Real Equipment Bonuses, and a New use Command', 'Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit item, the user''s explicit "full system" pick over two smaller options offered: equipment stat/AC affects, plus usable wands, staves, and scrolls. The seeded objaffect table already carried real per-item bonus data (stat rows, an AC row, an HP row, a Vitality row), and was already half-wired for weapon hit/damroll -- so the same table now feeds a worn item''s stat/AC/HP/Vitality bonus too, cached on the object at creation and applied/reversed on wear/remove. Two real things had to be caught by checking real data rather than assuming: (1) every real AC row in that table is negative, while Tobin''s own armor-class convention is the opposite (higher is better) -- import negates the sign; (2) an AC bonus initially only applied to items in the true "armor" category, silently dropping the bonus for a ring or other jewelry-category item that also carried a real AC row -- caught during live manual verification with a real seeded ring, then fixed so real AC data applies no matter what category the item collapsed into. For wands/staves/scrolls: the upstream val[] fields on these items turned out to be unreliable import noise (a comment on `identify` already flagged this from an earlier audit item -- a nonsense charge count on a real scroll), so charges/spell now live in a fresh Tobin-owned table instead of trusting that data. New `use <item> [target]` command: a scroll applies its effect once and is destroyed; a wand is charge-limited and targeted (a person in the room, or whoever you''re already fighting/yourself by default); a staff is charge-limited and always room-wide, hitting every other being present. The effect itself reuses the same heal/protective-ward/single-target-damage dispatch cast/pray already have, keyed off the stored spell''s own description, rather than a third copy of that logic. Deliberately not attempted this round: potions (a stretch for `use` -- would fit `drink`/`quaff` better, a separate command), a recharge mechanic for empty wands/staves (needs a future `edobject`-style tool), and mana costs (nothing in Tobin has a mana pool yet). New `tests/smoke_test_magic_items.py` (13 checks: real-data AC/HP affects applying and reverting cleanly on wear/remove, wand charges depleting and refusing further use, staff room-wide effect, scroll single-use destruction).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Object Maintenance, Part 1: Real Decay Timers and Combat-Broken Gear', 'Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit item, the user''s "full system" pick -- this session landed the first half (decay timers + combat structure damage); the repair-shop economy and per-class repair skills are carried to a follow-up session rather than rushed. The upstream `obj` table''s own real `decay` column was already being loaded but never actually used -- now a real ~60-second tick counts it down for anything sitting on a room floor (corpses, severed limbs, and now general room-floor objects), destroying it and relocating any contents to the room first when it hits zero. Caught a real hazard while building this, not shipped as one: that column''s schema default was 0 ("decays this tick"), which is backwards for any insert that does not mention it explicitly -- a good three dozen existing test fixtures do exactly that, and worse, our own zone-placed persistent world objects route through the identical creation path, so roughly five hundred real seeded rows with that same default would have started vanishing from the live world within about a minute of every zone reset. Fixed with a migration changing the default going forward, plus an explicit override at every zone-placement call site so persistent world content is never subject to it regardless. Separately: equipped gear can now actually break in a fight -- roughly one landed hit in three has a chance of wearing down whatever is worn on the limb that got hit, and at zero durability it is destroyed outright, any magical bonus it was granting reverses, and a ruined "scraps of" item is left behind in its place. Worth flagging for whoever picks up testing next: verifying this live turned up a real, reproducible test-harness quirk, not a game bug -- a test connection that sits open just polling for new output (no further commands sent) for 30+ seconds reliably gets disconnected when the test script driving it is run as a normal foreground SSH command, but the exact same connection pattern is fine when that same script is instead launched fully detached in the background and polled separately. If a live verification session ever sees a mysterious "lost its link" mid-test again, try that before assuming it is a real bug.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Object Maintenance, Part 2: Repair Skill and Repair-Shop Tickets', 'Closes out the Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit item Part 1 left half-done. Checked the real upstream `misc/repair.cc`/`disc/disc_warrior_blacksmithing.cc` first: a mature, file-backed ticket system with a real-time repair delay and genuinely per-MATERIAL repair skills (blacksmithing for metal, separate skills for organic/holy/magical/crystal/etc.). Tobin has no material-property system yet to gate that breadth on -- its own still-open audit item, deliberately not pulled forward just to unblock this one -- so this shipped Tobin-scale: one `repair` skill (Warrior, level 5) for self-repair, and a database-backed claim ticket instead of a physical note object, with no real-time delay (a repaired item at a shop is ready the moment you pay, not hours later). New: `player_inventory` now persists a carried item''s structure damage, depreciation, and monogram per-instance -- closes a real, latent gap where a damaged-but-not-destroyed worn item''s condition was silently reset to full on every reconnect since Session 55 shipped combat structure damage without a matching persistence column. Every repair (self or shop) permanently lowers how high an item can ever be restored again -- repeatedly patching the same piece of gear makes it a little more fragile each time, by design, same idea as the real upstream''s depreciation mechanic. A successful self-repair also stamps the item with the repairer''s name (cosmetic only for now -- not yet shown anywhere a player would see it, a known small gap, not an oversight). New commands documented in news.sql. Two bugs worth flagging for future reference: `db_query()` only supports %s/%i/%f format specifiers, not %li -- a `long` value always needs an explicit `(int)` cast, same as every other call site in the codebase already does, and it is an easy one to forget writing a brand-new repo file. Separately, `being_knows_skill()` gates any CLASS-tier skill purely on `basic_disc_pct > 0` plus level/class -- it does NOT check whether a `player_skill` row exists for that specific skill at all (that row only drives the proficiency roll once a skill is already unlocked). A test or admin script granting "knowledge" of a class skill by inserting a `player_skill` row alone, without also granting the discipline percentage, will not actually work -- caught live while writing this session''s own smoke test. New `tests/smoke_test_repair.py` (15 checks), run against the real seeded shop rather than a fabricated one. Deployed to production via a clean `copyover` the user ran from their own live session -- first deploy under the user''s new standing preference to verify on preview but deploy straight to production rather than keeping code running only on preview indefinitely.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Money System v2: Banking and Sales Tax', 'Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit item. Checked the real upstream first (`spec/spec_mobs_banker.cc`, `misc/shopowned.cc`/`shopaccounting.cc`, `docs/systems/critical/17-economy-system.md`): per-shop bank accounts, a fractional-reserve central bank (regular banks must hold a fraction of deposits in reserve at the central bank, withdrawals that would violate it are rejected), and sales tax scoped to player-OWNED shop transactions, journalized through a genuine double-entry ledger with a real chart of accounts. All of it entangled with a player-owned-shop/corporation economy Tobin doesn''t have. Asked before building rather than guessing: tax destination (a sink, a global treasury, or skip tax entirely) and bank scope (one global bank vs. per-shop accounts like the original) -- picked global treasury and single global bank. New `player_progress.bank_gold`, a second wallet: `bank`/`bank balance`/`bank deposit <amt>`/`bank withdraw <amt>` only work at a real shop flagged `is_bank`, seeded on shop_nr 4 ("Tobin City First Kingdom Bank", room 31750) -- picked over five other bank-named seeded rooms because most of those have `keeper == in_room` in the data, which looks like broken/unset import data rather than a real keeper mob. Interest: 0.5% once per real in-game day, applied as a single SQL UPDATE across every player row rather than a per-online-character loop, so offline balances accrue interest too. Sales tax: a flat 5% surcharge on ordinary `buy` purchases (hospital/stable/repair purchases are untouched -- they branch out of `cmd_buy()` before the tax code runs), collecting into a new singleton `world_treasury` row, visible to immortals via the new `treasury` command -- no spend mechanic yet, a disclosed gap and a hook for later rather than dead weight now. Command-table note: `bank` is deliberately placed after `bash`, not in strict alphabetical order, so the far-more-frequently-typed Warrior skill keeps the "ba" abbreviation -- same precedent as `retrieve` after `return`. New `tests/smoke_test_bank.py` (17 checks) caught a bug in the test itself, not the feature: the first draft bought a 3-gold torch, whose 5% tax rounds down to 0 and so never triggers the tax message at all (`cmd_buy()`''s own `if (tax > 0)` guard) -- would have passed for the wrong reason. Switched to a pricier item so the tax path is actually exercised. Daily interest itself isn''t covered by the automated test (a real in-game day is ~96 real minutes at Tobin''s default clock speed, the same "not practical to keep automated" call already made for `smoke_test_heartbeat.py`''s own real-time boundary) -- sanity-checked the UPDATE query and its FLOOR() rounding manually instead.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'Material Properties, Real Condition Wording, and No More Damage Numbers', 'Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit item (material properties) plus a cluster of related polish that came up live while testing it. MATERIALS: checked the real upstream first (`misc/materials.h`/`.cc`) -- its own doc claims direct weapon-damage/armor-AC multiplier formulas that do NOT actually exist in the shipped code; what''s real is durability (mutual hardness-vs-hardness wear) and value (a flat weight x material-price lookup). Asked before building: port only the real mechanics, or also add a genuine damage/AC multiplier (a disclosed invention beyond what Sneezy actually does)? User picked the invention. Reused Tobin''s EXISTING real seeded `obj.material` column (populated since earlier object-affects work, never mechanically read until now) rather than adding a new field -- bucketed the 83 real MAT_* IDs into 5 Tobin-scale tiers (Common/Fine/Superior/Rare/Legendary, new `material.c`) instead of porting all 83. Each tier scales damage, AC, a max_struct bonus at creation (feeds the repair-shop economy''s ceiling too), and shop value. `identify` shows a new Material: line. CONDITION TEXT: user, after seeing invented wording in a test run: "put the condition of items after the short desc. search sneezy for like new." Found the real `TObj::equip_condition()` (misc/info.cc) -- an 11-tier ladder with real per-tier ANSI colors, ported verbatim. Shown inline after short_descr in inventory/equipment (e.g. "a long sword (brand new)", colorized) and as a new Condition: row in identify. DAMAGE NUMBERS: the 2026-07-12 "dont report damage" decision only ever covered mortal-visible melee text -- an immortal-visible branch and every spell/trap/wand-staff damage message still printed the raw number. User: "take out the damage number and use it to describe how hard the hit was" -- confirmed via AskUserQuestion this should apply everywhere. Ported the real `describe_dam()` (misc/combat.cc): an 11-tier ladder from "pathetically" to "into shreds"/"into a bloody pulp", compared against the struck limb''s CURRENT pre-hit HP (not max), so the same raw number reads more brutal against an already-battered limb. LOAD OBJ: user: "when an immort loads an obj let it go into inventory rather than the room" -- simple on its face, but broke two existing tests that load as one immortal and `get` as a different character (now fixed to `drop` explicitly first); the other ~55 test files using `load obj` were NOT audited, flagged as a real risk for the next full sweep. DB REGRESSIONS: found and fixed three "applied live only, never migrated" content fixes that Home''s own database had silently never received despite being marked done -- Grimhaven->Tobin City (639 rooms), SneezyMUD->TobinMUD (4 rows), and talens->gold (263 shop rows, user caught this one live: "That''ll be 198 talens. should be gold"). All three now captured as idempotent migrations so they can''t silently regress again. New `tests/smoke_test_material.py` (12 checks). Also: a process-restart command of mine accidentally killed production instead of just preview mid-session (disclosed immediately; user said not to worry about reboots at this stage) -- since then, killing by exact PID only, and preview retired entirely per user request ("stop running preview, do all work on production").')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New: Sign Language', 'Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit item. Checked the real upstream doc first (its own communication-system writeup): silent, room-only speech that only characters who also know sign language actually read -- everyone else just gets a vague "makes funny motions with hands" line, with one deliberate carve-out ported straight from the original: a Thief signer is always understood by anyone, full stop, since the original treats hand-talk as part of the underworld''s common repertoire rather than a trained skill for that class specifically. New `sign <message>` command (cmd_sign.c) and a new `sign` skill, added identically to every class''s roster (same "everyone gets it" precedent already established for `riding`) rather than gated to one class, since the original lists it under its general "adventuring" discipline rather than any specific class list. Gated on the same basics the original itself checks: not fighting, not asleep, both hands empty, neither arm hurt (reusing the real 20% "hurt" threshold the limb-display code already uses) -- deliberately NOT the original''s exact minimum-position check, since Tobin''s position field is never actually driven to the specific value the original requires, so porting that literally would have been a silent no-op rather than a real gate.')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New: Drug Tracking (pipeweed, opium, pot, frogslime)', 'Sneezy -> Tobin feature audit item. Checked the real upstream doc first (its own drug-tracking writeup): consumption applies a real temporary stat effect, tracked for addiction (a genuine lifetime average consumption rate) and withdrawal (a real penalty once you go too long without a dose past that drug''s own real threshold). The original''s stat effects lean on six attributes Tobin''s simplified system doesn''t model at all (bravery, agility, focus, speed, perception, karma), so every drug''s effect here is a deliberate remap onto Tobin''s real six stats rather than a literal port -- documented field-by-field in the new drug.h. Two things were deliberately NOT ported as-is: the original''s own opium effect, which its own documentation flags as outright buggy (checks one stat, sets a different one) -- replaced with a clean, internally consistent penalty instead of reproducing the bug; and frogslime''s real speech-garbling effect, since Tobin has no drunk/garble speech mechanic anywhere yet to hook it into (a separate, bigger lift) -- kept as flavor plus a real chance of being knocked out cold instead. New `smoke <item>` command, a new `drug` item keyword (same generic-by-keyword convention as spell components and holy symbols), and four new example items. One design note for whoever touches this next: an active dose''s own effect window uses a real tick countdown (not wall-clock time) specifically so `aitick` can force it to expire deterministically in a test, matching the same convention corpse decay already uses -- but withdrawal genuinely depends on real elapsed time since last use, so testing that instead relies on SQL-setting a fake last-use timestamp before forcing a single tick, not a real wait. New `tests/smoke_test_drugs.py` (10 checks).')
INSERT INTO `wiznews` (`author`, `title`, `body`) VALUES
('The TobinMUD Team', 'New: `edit object` (oedit) -- the object-prototype editor', 'TODO.md''s "NEXT UP" item, the last piece of the builder-tools-OLC audit gap (edroom/edzone/edplayer already existed; objects and mobs were the two still missing). Ported the real upstream''s own `update_obj_menu()` (misc/create_objs.cc) field-for-field for familiarity, renumbered sequentially (1-17) rather than preserving its 1-8/10-21 gaps -- Tobin''s other editors don''t preserve upstream slot numbers either. Same menu-driven working-copy pattern as edzone: loads a DB snapshot on entry (obj_proto_load()), field edits mutate the copy only, (S)ave writes the whole row back in one query (obj_proto_save()), (Q)uit with unsaved changes prompts Save/Discard/Cancel. EDIT-ONLY, same scope boundary edroom draws around rooms -- there is no way to allocate a brand-new vnum here (a separate concern, like `dig` is separate from `edit room`). Take Flags / Extra Flags (wear_flag/action_flag) open a toggle-by-number submenu, same shape as edroom''s room-flags submenu, backed by two new accessor pairs in obj.h (obj_wear_flag_count/name, obj_action_flag_count/name) over the existing display-only name tables. Three real gaps in this pass, all disclosed rather than silent: an item''s `action_desc` (the original''s own menu labels this slot "9) Unused" with NO case in its own dispatcher either -- genuinely not exposed there, not a Tobin omission), "Extra Description" (needs an objextra-style per-object extra-desc table Tobin doesn''t have, unlike rooms'' roomextra), and "Applys" (the objaffect stat/AC bonus rows -- its own related-table submenu, also wizpower-gated at 53+ in the original, a granular permission system Tobin doesn''t have and isn''t replicating as a separate gate here). No zone_can_edit() check, unlike edroom/edzone -- object prototypes have no zone association in the schema at all, so there''s no ownership boundary to enforce; gated at BUILD_MIN_LEVEL only. New `tests/smoke_test_edobject.py` (17 checks) verified live against a real sandbox prototype row -- menu display, dirty-before-save, flag toggling, four-values editing, Save persisting everything in one write, a nonexistent-vnum rejection, and Discard genuinely discarding.')
